<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pikillacta</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pikillacta is a large Wari archaeological site in the Lucre basin about 30 kilometres south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cusco">Cusco</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>. Built from around 600 CE as a provincial centre of the Wari Empire, it consists of hundreds of rectangular enclosures laid out on a rigid grid and covering about 2 square kilometres. It was abandoned around 1000 CE, long before the rise of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inca-empire">Incas</a> in the Cusco region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pikillacta.jpg" alt="Pikillacta" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AgainErick · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Piquillacta_Archaeological_site_-_street.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
